# Machine owner file for the "gplay (Play Console CLI)" row in TOOLCHAIN.md.
# Format: `key=value`, `#` comments. Consumed by tools/bootstrap, tools/doctor,
# tools/play and the `pins` lane of ./tools/check fast.
#
# gplay is https://github.com/tamtom/play-console-cli — a single Go binary, MIT
# licensed, that speaks nine documented Google APIs. Decision 0142 section 9
# records why it is used instead of the repository-owned client that preceded
# it, and what this repository still owns.
#
# WHY A CHECKSUM AND NOT ONLY A VERSION
#
# This is the first tool this repository pins that (a) holds a credential able
# to publish to the public store and (b) ships a self-update path. `gplay
# update` replaces the binary in place, and it checks for a new version on
# every startup unless told not to. A tool that can rewrite itself between two
# runs is exactly what "this repository installs nothing unpinned at run time"
# exists to refuse, so the version alone is not the pin: the SHA-256 of the
# exact release asset for each host is, and ./tools/play verifies the binary it
# is about to run against the line for this host before handing it anything.
#
# Consequently GPLAY_NO_UPDATE=1 is set by ./tools/play on every invocation.
# Upgrading is an edit to this file plus a fresh `./tools/bootstrap`, which is
# how every other pin here moves.
#
# GPLAY_NO_STAR_PROMPT=1 is also set. After a successful metadata push the tool
# prints a suggestion that the agent driving it ask the person to star its
# GitHub repository. It is polite about it — it never waits for input and never
# stars anything itself — but a prompt addressed at an assistant, arriving
# through tool output, asking for an action on the operator's own account, is
# not an instruction this repository's tooling should carry into a release log.
# Suppressing it also keeps the audit trail free of a line nobody asked for.
#
# Pinned 2026-09-08 to the current release. Checksums are the published
# checksums.txt of that tag, recorded here rather than fetched, so that a
# rewritten release asset is a mismatch instead of a silent upgrade.

